Third-party components
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ViralBench ships no third-party source of its own: everything below is resolved
and installed by the user's package manager (uv/pip, npm) or downloaded as a
released binary at setup time. Nothing here is vendored into this repository.
The authoritative dependency lists are `pyproject.toml` (Python),
`config/crowd-requirements.txt` (the optional crowd simulation environment) and
`tooling/browser-mcp/package.json` (the founder's browser tooling).

Run-time tools invoked as subprocesses (not linked, not redistributed)
----------------------------------------------------------------------

OpenCode CLI
  https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode
  MIT License.
  The founder stage drives OpenCode as a coding agent. It is executed as a
  separate process (`opencode run`, see `viral_bench.founder.harness`), never
  imported or bundled. `scripts/setup_founder_env.sh` downloads the official
  release binary to `~/.opencode/bin` on request; this project distributes no
  copy of it.

Playwright MCP (@playwright/mcp)
  https://github.com/microsoft/playwright-mcp
  Apache License 2.0.
  Gives the founder's QA and Designer agents a real browser. Only the pinned
  version in `tooling/browser-mcp/package.json` plus its lock file are committed;
  `tooling/browser-mcp/bootstrap.sh` installs it into a gitignored
  `node_modules/`, and the tooling silently disables itself if it is absent.

Google Chrome / Chromium
  Not distributed here. Both the crowd's browser trials and the rubric checks
  drive whatever system Chrome or Chromium the user already has, via
  Playwright's `chrome` channel — no browser binary is downloaded or shipped by
  this project.

Python dependencies
-------------------

The direct dependencies in `pyproject.toml` are permissively licensed:
Apache-2.0 (google-genai, playwright, python-multipart, bcrypt), BSD-3-Clause
(python-dotenv, uvicorn, starlette, httpx, jinja2, flask, markdown, reportlab,
passlib), MIT (pyyaml, fastapi, pydantic, python-jose, pyjwt, alembic,
sqlalchemy, gunicorn), HPND (pillow) and Unlicense (email-validator).

Two transitive dependencies are weak-copyleft, and are noted here because that
is worth knowing before redistributing an environment built from this project:

  certifi — MPL-2.0. Pulled in by httpx and requests; unavoidable, and present
    in essentially every Python HTTP stack. Used unmodified.
  tqdm — MPL-2.0 AND MIT. Reached only through the optional `crowd` extra
    (camel-ai -> openai -> tqdm); it is not part of the base install.

Optional crowd simulation extra
-------------------------------

The social simulation is optional and is not installed by default.

CAMEL (camel-ai) and OASIS (camel-oasis)
  https://github.com/camel-ai/camel and https://github.com/camel-ai/oasis
  Apache License 2.0.
  CAMEL provides the agent framework the crowd's `SocialAgent`s are built on;
  OASIS provides the mock social platform they act in. `camel-ai` is the
  `crowd` extra in `pyproject.toml` (`uv sync --extra crowd`); the full
  `camel-oasis` engine is pinned in `config/crowd-requirements.txt` and is
  installed by `scripts/setup_crowd_env.sh` into a separate Python 3.11
  virtualenv (`.venv-crowd`), because it cannot coexist with this project's
  Python 3.12 dependency set.

  IMPORTANT — GPL: installing `camel-oasis` also pulls in **igraph**
  (python-igraph), which is licensed **GPL-2.0-or-later**. ViralBench does not
  distribute, vendor, link against or import igraph. It reaches a machine only
  when the user chooses to run `scripts/setup_crowd_env.sh`, and it lands in the
  isolated `.venv-crowd` environment rather than in this project's own
  environment. If GPL code in your environment is a problem for you, the crowd
  simulation is the part to leave uninstalled — the rest of the benchmark, the
  founder stage and the scoring included, does not need it.

Container image
---------------

`docker/Containerfile` builds a local sandbox image for running generated apps.
It derives from the official `python` Debian image and installs Node.js from
NodeSource and a small set of Debian packages at build time. Those components
keep their own licenses; no image is published from this repository.
